{"description":"Control: identical state and parameters, never stimulated. How much of the phosphate rise is the initial condition rather than the exercise is measured, not asserted: index.rest_control carries the fraction and both arms' numbers.","expt":3,"id":"rest_only","protocol":{"cycle_s":0.65,"freq_hz":0.0,"repetitions":0,"stim_s":0.1625,"t_exercise_s":6.5,"t_recovery_s":6.5},"provenance":{"archive_version":"zenodo:10.5281/zenodo.15485446 v1.1.0. INCOMPLETE: five of the ten Data/*.mat files the published scripts load are absent from the repository, so the manuscript's own figures cannot be regenerated from it. REFERENCES.md records when that was checked.","confidence":"educational_approximation","evidence_type":"Derived","fitted_to":"Mouse fast-twitch myofibres. The authors' words: 'we fit our model to voltage and to myoplasmic Ca2+ data collected from multiple papers that conducted fluorescence or voltage measurements in single mouse fast-twitch myofibers', and 'we approximate the muscle fiber as a cylinder of radius 20 um, matching the size of mouse fast twitch fibers'. They flag the gap themselves: 'new datasets are needed to reliably calibrate models of human myofibers at physiological temperatures over extended times'. NOTHING HERE RESCALES THAT. Which human muscle this run is shown under is OURS and is a navigation choice, not a claim that the muscle named in the breadcrumb behaves like this.","inputs":{"cycle_s":0.65,"expt":3,"repetitions":0,"stimulation_frequency_hz":0.0,"stimulation_s":0.1625,"t_exercise_s":6.5,"t_recovery_s":6.5},"integrator":"SciPy solve_ivp(method='BDF'), rtol=0.001 atol=1e-06 \u2014 NOT MATLAB ode15s. The NonNegative option has no SciPy equivalent and is not emulated: nothing is clamped.","known_discrepancy":"10-12% NRMSE against the digitised experimental data. This is the published fit's own residual, not a porting error \u2014 it is printed in the authors' own figures (REPORT.md \u00a75a).","model_id":"francis_skelmuscleca","model_version":"SkelMuscleCa_dydt.m, the no-mitochondria model. Upstream RangamaniLabUCSD/SkelMuscleCa @ 5e94ba1fa7c983f170a56741e9711432143d6d71. The preprint DOI is deliberately version-less above: REFERENCES.md links v1 and calls it preprint v3, the port was read against v1/v2, and nothing in this repository pins which rendering of the manuscript is authoritative.","paper_id":"biorxiv:10.1101/2025.05.22.655415","parameters":"Data/p0Struct.mat x Data/pSol_allParam_noMito.mat, unmodified. Modifications applied, both the authors' own: p(91)=0 (DHPR off) and p(12)=p(12)*cSR0, per SkelMuscleObj.m:104,221.","port_source_sha256":"6611127f841cb0a80c96c9157fd1eacee93a9531dfa937020f4dd6c3799f0d98","read_rest_only_first":"Both steady states are solved with the authors' SSPhosAccum = false (SkelMuscleCa_MakeFigs.m:169, 'always false in tests for this paper'), so myoplasmic phosphate starts away from its own equilibrium and climbs even at rest. Myoplasmic phosphate rises 5048 uM over the 6.5 s of exercise. The SAME state and parameters left unstimulated (rest_only) rise 2015 uM over the same span, so 40% of the accumulation happens with no stimulus at all -- it is the initial condition relaxing, not the bout. This matters because phosphate is what the authors attribute the force decline to ('this effect was largely attributed to phosphate accumulation in the myoplasm'), so a reader taking the rise as the cost of the exercise would over-read it by that fraction. The remaining 3033 uM is the bout's.","scenario":"rest_only","source":"Francis et al., bioRxiv 2025.05.22.655415 v1/v2 \u2014 the no-mitochondria model (SkelMuscleCa_dydt.m), fitted to mouse fast-twitch fibres. The with-mito variant is not ported.","steady_state":"Resting SR 930.5-941.2 uM in these scenarios, ~1.9x the objective's 500 uM soft-penalty target. The authors' Figure 5A reads ~855 uM, so this port lands about 10% high; that gap is NOT reproduced agreement and is unexplained (REPORT.md \u00a75d). Figure 5A's own generator cannot be run from the repository \u2014 it calls the with-mitochondria model with a parameter vector that was never published.","time_unit":"seconds","uncertainty":"NO BAND SHIPS AND THE AUTHORS DRAW NONE. Their published uncertainty for these trajectories is a fit residual, not a distribution: 10-12% NRMSE against the digitised experimental data, printed in their own figures (see known_discrepancy and REPORT.md 5a). There is no ensemble behind this line to draw a band from -- it is one integration at the published parameters, and the residual says how far that fit stands from the data it was fitted to. Read it as the fit, not as a mean.","validation":"Right-hand side bit-identical to the published model across 198,656 values; trajectory matches the published figure curves to <=1.7% on peaks; trajectory not verified against the authors' solver (MATLAB ode15s), which nothing here runs. Verified against the authors' unmodified code under GNU Octave's ode23s instead (REPORT.md 5e), from the same steady state and parameters over 1 s at 100 Hz: calcium peak +0.38%, force +0.02% (NRMSE 0.013%), SR store 0.00%, worst of 51 states 1.92% NRMSE (extracellular K). That steady state is an exact zero of the authors' own right-hand side. Two integrators agreeing on one right-hand side is what this can say; it is not their solver.","whose_protocol":"cycle_s and stim_s are the authors' exerciseParam (SkelMuscleCa_MakeFigs.m:141) and freq_hz is their stimulation frequency for the no-mito model (:184). THE PAIRING IS PUBLISHED TWICE AND IS NOT OURS. Linden-Santangeli et al. (2025), driving this very model, state it outright in their methods: 'The running stimulus was 0.1625 seconds of 100 Hz activation for each 0.65-second stride, repeated for a total of 60 seconds' (doi:10.1101/2025.06.02.657503). Francis et al.'s own Figure 8C caption agrees -- 'patterns of 100 Hz stimuli designed to mimic gastrocnemius medialis activity during running' -- while the methods paragraph defining the same stride says 70 or 120 Hz and the script sides with the text (:1222, freq = 120, with mitochondria); the one live-code pairing at 100 Hz, :171, is commented out. Two papers publish the pairing and one of them contradicts itself. WHAT IS OURS IS THE LENGTH: repetitions, t_exercise_s and t_recovery_s. The published length for THIS stimulus is 60 seconds -- Linden-Santangeli in the sentence above, and Francis's own running test is '20 s of running' while '10 repetitions (60 s total)' is their RESISTANCE set on its 6 s cycle. Ten repetitions of a 0.65 s stride is 6.5 s: a tenth of the length the cell scale's own paper ran this exact stimulus for. No published run of either holds a recovery segment at all."},"sampling":{"head_samples":0,"note":"the first `head_samples` archived samples, then every `stride`th, rounded. No interpolation or smoothing, and the resulting grid is NOT uniform \u2014 search `t`.","round_digits":5,"source":"scenarios/data/rest_only.json","stride":18},"series":{"Ca_SR_total":[941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.154,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.155,941.1